[Event "vs 1...d5: 2.c4"]

{White hits d5 from the flank with c4 instead of supporting it with d4. Black must hold with ...e6 or ...c6, advance with ...d4, or capture with ...dxc4.}
1. Nf3 {[%csl Ge5,Gd4] The Réti starts flexibly. The knight controls e5 and d4 and stops ...e5 without committing a centre pawn, so White keeps c4, g3, b3, d4 or e4 in reserve.}
1... d5 {[%csl Ge4,Gc4] The classical reply: Black grabs the centre and controls e4 and c4. The d5-pawn can also become a target.}
2. c4 {[%cal Gc4d5] The signature Réti move. The c-pawn strikes d5 from the side and asks Black how to deal with the tension.}
2... e6 {[%cal Ge6d5] A solid Queen's-Gambit-style answer. It holds d5, but the c8-bishop is now locked behind the pawn chain.}
(2... c6 {Slav-style: Black holds d5 and keeps the c8-bishop free to come out to f5 or g4.}
 3. b3 {[%cal Gb3c4] Supports c4, so ...dxc4 can be met by bxc4, and prepares Bb2.}
 3... Nf6 {Develops and adds a guard to d5 and e4. The knight on f6 controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
 4. g3 {Heading for the double fianchetto: both bishops will rake the centre from long range.}
 4... Bf5 {[%cal Gf5b1] The point of ...c6: the bishop develops outside the pawn chain before ...e6.}
 5. Bg2 {[%cal Gg2d5] The bishop adds pressure to d5 and the long diagonal.}
 5... e6 {Completes Black's ideal "London-style" structure: pawns on c6, d5, e6.}
 6. Bb2 {[%cal Gb2f6] The second bishop aims at the long diagonal toward f6 and the kingside.}
 6... Nbd7 {Supports ...e5 or ...c5 later and keeps the position compact.}
 7. O-O {King safety first. White's plan is d3, Nbd2 and a timely e4 or cxd5.}
 7... h6 {[%csl Gh7] Makes a retreat square on h7 for the f5-bishop, so Nh4 can't trap or exchange it cheaply.}
 8. d3 {[%csl Ge4] Takes e4 away from Black's pieces and blunts the f5-bishop. Nbd2 and e4 are coming.}
 8... Be7 {Modest but solid development, preparing to castle.}
 9. Nbd2 {[%cal Gd2e4] The knight supports the e4 break, the key plan in this structure.}
 9... O-O {Both sides are developed. White plays for e4 or for queenside expansion with a3 and b4.})
(2... c6 3. b3 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] The bishop pressures f3 and Black prepares ...Nd7 and ...e5.}
 4. Bb2 {[%cal Gb2e5] Controls e5, the square Black wants for a pawn break.}
 4... Nd7 {Supports ...e5. Leaving f6 free keeps the f7-pawn mobile too.}
 5. g3 {Fianchetto. The g2-bishop will bear down on d5.}
 5... Ngf6 {Develops and guards e4. The knight on f6 controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
 6. Bg2 {The long diagonal is now loaded. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 6... e6 {Solid. Black builds a pawn triangle and develops the f8-bishop next.}
 7. O-O {White castles and prepares d3 and h3, asking the g4-bishop to commit.}
 7... Bd6 {[%cal Gd6h2] The bishop aims at h2, and it also supports a ...e5 push.}
 8. d3 {Controls e4 and keeps the b2-bishop's diagonal solid. Nbd2 and h3 follow.}
 8... O-O {Typical plans: White plays h3, Nbd2 and e4, or cxd5 to open the c-file.})
(2... d4 {[%csl Gd4] Black gains space and cramps c3. It's a reversed Benoni with White a tempo up.}
 3. g3 {Calm and flexible: the bishop goes to g2, and b4 or e3 will undermine d4 later.}
 3... Nc6 {Supports d4 and prepares ...e5. Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
 4. Bg2 {[%cal Gg2b7] Bites on the long diagonal toward b7 and the queenside.}
 4... e5 {Black builds a big d4/e5 centre: space, but also targets.}
 5. d3 {[%csl Ge4] Fixes e4 as White's square and prepares e3 or b4 to undermine the d4-pawn.}
 5... Nf6 {Develops and keeps an eye on e4. The knight on f6 controls e4 and d5.}
 6. O-O {King safety. Now e3 is the main lever. Castles kingside: the king is safe and the rook on f1 joins the game.}
 6... Be7 {A modest but safe home for the bishop: it shields the f6-knight from pins and lets Black castle next. Don't worry that it looks passive; it can reroute later.}
 7. e3 {[%cal Ge3d4] Hits d4 straight away. The tension forces Black to choose how to keep the centre.}
 7... O-O {Black allows exchanges on d4. Castles kingside: the king is safe and the rook on f8 joins the game.}
 8. exd4 {Opens the e-file so a rook can attack the e-pawn or the d-pawn.}
 8... exd4 {[%csl Rd4] Black keeps the wedge, but it's now an isolated, advanced pawn. Re1, Bf4 and b4-b5 put pressure on it.})
(2... d4 3. e3 {[%cal Ge3d4] The direct approach: challenge the d4-pawn at once.}
 3... Nc6 {Defends d4 with a piece. Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
 4. exd4 {Releases the tension and opens lines for White's pieces.}
 4... Nxd4 {Recaptures with the knight, which is centralised but can be swapped off.}
 5. Nxd4 {Exchanges the strong knight. Black's queen has to recapture and comes out early.}
 5... Qxd4 {[%csl Rd4] The queen is central but exposed: White will develop with tempo.}
 6. Nc3 {Develops and eyes b5 and d5. The knight on c3 controls d5 and e4.}
 6... c6 {Takes b5 and d5 away from the c3-knight. Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and d5.}
 7. d3 {[%cal Gc1e3] Opens the c1-bishop's diagonal so Be3 comes with tempo.}
 7... Nf6 {Develops and covers d5 and e4. The knight on f6 controls e4 and d5.}
 8. Be3 {[%cal Ge3d4] Hits the queen again. White gains another tempo and leads in development.}
 8... Qd7 {The queen retreats. White is comfortably ahead in development, and Be2/O-O and d4 follow.})
(2... dxc4 {Black gives up the centre. The pawn is usually easy to win back.}
 3. e3 {[%cal Gf1c4] Opens the f1-bishop so it can recapture on c4.}
 3... Nf6 {Develops. Holding the c4-pawn with ...b5 would just weaken Black's queenside.}
 4. Bxc4 {Pawn regained. The structure resembles a Queen's Gambit Accepted, with a nicely active bishop.}
 4... e6 {Solid. Black opens the f8-bishop. Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the bishop on f8.}
 5. O-O {King safety. White can choose between d4 and a b3/Bb2 setup.}
 5... c5 {Black strikes at the centre, the standard freeing move.}
 6. b3 {Prepares Bb2 for the long diagonal and keeps d4 in reserve.}
 6... Nc6 {Develops and adds control of d4 and e5. The knight on c6 controls d4 and e5.}
 7. Bb2 {[%cal Gb2g7] The long diagonal points at Black's kingside.}
 7... a6 {Prepares ...b5 to gain time on the c4-bishop.}
 8. a4 {[%csl Rb5] Stops ...b5 for good and secures c4 for the bishop. The cost is a hole on b4.})
(2... dxc4 3. Na3 {The knight heads for c4 to recapture and later eyes e5 and b6.}
 3... c5 {Black stakes a claim to d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
 4. Nxc4 {Regains the pawn with a well-placed knight. Takes the pawn on c4, and it cannot be taken back at once.}
 4... Nc6 {Develops and controls d4 and e5. The knight on c6 controls d4 and e5.}
 5. g3 {Fianchetto toward the long diagonal. The pawn on g3 controls f4 and h4.}
 5... f6 {[%csl Rg6,Re6] Black prepares ...e5 for a big pawn centre. It's solid, but it weakens the light squares around the king.}
 6. Bg2 {The light-squared bishop will exploit those light-square weaknesses.}
 6... e5 {Black's centre: lots of space, but the d5 and e6 squares and the a2–g8 diagonal are soft.}
 7. O-O {White prepares d3, Be3 and Qb3 or a4, aiming at the light squares.})
(2... Nf6 {Develops and guards d5 while keeping the c8-bishop free.}
 3. cxd5 {Trades the flank pawn for Black's central d-pawn, a classical hypermodern swap.}
 3... Nxd5 {Recaptures, but the knight in the centre can be kicked.}
 4. g3 {Fianchetto aiming at the d5-knight and the long diagonal.}
 4... g6 {Black mirrors White with a fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
 5. Bg2 {Pressure on d5 and b7. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 5... Bg7 {Black contests the long diagonal. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 6. O-O {White castles before deciding the centre.}
 6... O-O {Symmetrical, but it's White to move. Castles kingside: the king is safe and the rook on f8 joins the game.}
 7. Nc3 {Develops with tempo on the d5-knight. The knight on c3 controls e4 and eyes the knight on d5.}
 7... Nb6 {Steps away from exchanges. The knight eyes c4.}
 8. d3 {Flexible: controls c4 and e4. Be3 and Qc1/Bh6 or a4-a5 ideas follow.})
3. g3 {[%cal Gf1g2] Preparing the fianchetto. The bishop on g2 will pressure d5 along the long diagonal.}
3... Nf6 {Natural. The knight guards d5 and e4. Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
(3... d4 {[%csl Gd4] Black gains space and cramps White's queenside knight.}
 4. d3 {[%csl Ge4] Fixes e4 for White and prepares e3 or b4 to undermine d4.}
 4... Nc6 {Defends d4 and eyes b4 and e5. Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
 5. Bg2 {[%cal Gg2b7] The bishop aims at b7 and the queenside. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 5... Nf6 {Develops and covers e4. The knight on f6 controls e4 and d5.}
 6. O-O {King safety. e3 is the lever. Castles kingside: the king is safe and the rook on f1 joins the game.}
 6... e5 {Black builds a broad centre: space, but also targets.}
 7. e3 {[%cal Ge3d4] Attacks the d4-pawn immediately. Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 controls f4 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
 7... Be7 {Calm. Black allows the exchange. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 8. exd4 {Opens the e-file. Takes the pawn on d4; Black will want to recapture.}
 8... exd4 {[%csl Rd4] Black keeps a wedge on d4, but it's isolated. Re1, Bf4 and b4 will target it.})
4. Bg2 {Completes the fianchetto: steady pressure on d5 and the long diagonal.}
4... Be7 {Solid, preparing to castle. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
(4... dxc4 {Black grabs the c4-pawn while it's undefended. In the Réti this is usually temporary.}
 5. Qa4+ {[%cal Ga4c4,Ga4e8] The point: a check that also attacks c4, so White wins the pawn back with tempo.}
 5... Nbd7 {Blocks the check and develops. The knight on d7 controls e5.}
 6. Qxc4 {Pawn regained. The queen on c4 and the bishop on g2 both eye Black's queenside.}
 6... a6 {Prepares ...b5 to kick the queen and gain queenside space.}
 7. Qc2 {[%csl Ge4] Leaves before ...b5 hits it. From c2 the queen watches e4.}
 7... c5 {Black frees the position and claims d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
 8. O-O {Castles. White plans d3/Nbd2/e4 or b3/Bb2. Castles kingside: the king is safe and the rook on f1 joins the game.}
 8... b6 {Prepares ...Bb7 to contest the long diagonal.}
 9. d3 {Controls e4 and c4 and prepares Nbd2 and e4. Supports the centre: the pawn on d3 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1.})
5. O-O {King safety. White now chooses between b3/Bb2 and d4.}
5... O-O {Black castles too, finishing kingside development.}
6. b3 {[%cal Gc1b2] The double-fianchetto Réti: the b2-bishop will aim down the long diagonal toward e5 and g7.}
6... c5 {Black gains central space and frees c6 for the knight.}
(6... b6 {Black mirrors White's plan to contest the long diagonal.}
 7. Bb2 {[%cal Gb2f6] Both of White's bishops are now trained on the centre.}
 7... Bb7 {Black matches the long-diagonal bishop. Fianchettoes the bishop on b7, where it rakes the long diagonal through c6 and d5.}
 8. e3 {Supports a later d4 and keeps the structure flexible.}
 8... c5 {Space in the centre. The pawn on c5 controls b4 and d4.}
 9. Nc3 {Develops and adds pressure on d5. The knight on c3 controls e4 and eyes the pawn on d5.}
 9... Nc6 {Develops, controlling d4 and e5. The knight on c6 controls d4 and e5.}
