11. d4 {[%cal Gd4c5,Gb2d4] Attacks c5 at once. If Black plays ...c4, White gets the b3 lever and e5. Otherwise dxc5 leaves an isolated d-pawn.} *

[Event "vs 1...d5: 2.g3 (King's Indian Attack)"]

{White builds a King's Indian setup with g3, Bg2, O-O, d3, Nbd2 and e4. The plan is a kingside attack with e5, h4 and Nf1-h2-g4, or a central e4 break.}
1. Nf3 {[%csl Ge5,Gd4] A flexible start that keeps every system available.}
1... d5 {Black takes the centre. The pawn on d5 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
2. g3 {[%cal Gf1g2] The King's Indian Attack. White fianchettoes first and aims for e4 later.}
2... Nf6 {Natural development. The knight on f6 controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
(2... c6 {Prepares ...Bg4 with the d5-pawn solidly supported.}
 3. Bg2 {Fianchetto. The bishop on g2 rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 3... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] The bishop develops actively and pins against e2 and d1.}
 4. O-O {King safety, then d3/Nbd2 and e4. Castles kingside: the king is safe and the rook on f1 joins the game.}
 4... Nd7 {Supports ...e5 to build a big centre. Develops the knight to d7, where it controls e5.}
 5. d3 {[%csl Ge4] Prepares e4. Supports the centre: the pawn on d3 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1.}
 5... Ngf6 {Black's knight goes to f6 rather than d7 so it guards e4 and d5. With the g4-bishop pinning f3, Black hopes to meet e4 with ...dxe4 and easy play.}
 6. Nbd2 {[%cal Gd2e4] Supports e4. Develops the knight to d2, where it controls e4.}
 6... e5 {Black has the ideal centre with pawns on c6, d5 and e5.}
 7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] White's thematic break, challenging d5. Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls f5 and eyes the pawn on d5.}
 7... Be7 {A modest but safe home for the bishop: it shields the f6-knight from pins and lets Black castle next. Don't worry that it looks passive; it can reroute later.}
 8. h3 {Asks the bishop to decide: exchange on f3 or retreat.}
 8... Bh5 {Keeps the pin, but the bishop could get stranded after g4.}
 9. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5] The rook backs up e4 and puts indirect pressure on e5.})
(2... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Black develops the bishop early, before ...e6.}
 3. Bg2 {Fianchetto. The bishop on g2 rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 3... Nd7 {Prepares ...e5 and ...Ngf6. Develops the knight to d7, where it controls e5.}
 4. O-O {White castles first and keeps d3/d4 in reserve. Don't rush h3: the g4-bishop isn't doing much yet, and kicking it now only helps Black decide where it belongs.}
 4... c6 {Supports d5. Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and supports the pawn on d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
 5. d3 {Prepares e4. Supports the centre: the pawn on d3 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1.}
 5... e5 {Black's centre is impressive, but White aims to undermine it.}
 6. Nbd2 {Supports e4. Develops the knight to d2, where it controls e4.}
 6... Ngf6 {The knight comes to f6 to hold e4 once White pushes it. Black has the classical centre; White's job is to break it up with e4 and a timely exf5 or exd5.}
 7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] Challenges d5 and opens lines for the g2-bishop.}
 7... Be7 {A modest but safe home for the bishop: it shields the f6-knight from pins and lets Black castle next. Don't worry that it looks passive; it can reroute later.}
 8. h3 {Asks the g4-bishop to commit. The pawn on h3 eyes the bishop on g4.}
 8... Bh5 {Keeps the pin. Moves the bishop out of attack to h5.})
(2... c5 {A reversed King's Indian: Black plays in the centre aggressively.}
 3. Bg2 {Fianchetto. The bishop on g2 rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 3... Nc6 {Develops and supports ...e5. The knight on c6 controls d4 and e5.}
 4. O-O {Castling first keeps every option open: c4 to hit d5, or d3 and e4 to take on Black's big centre later.}
 4... e5 {[%csl Gd4] Black has a big centre. White aims to undermine it with c3, e4 and Re1.}
 5. d3 {Prepares e4 and keeps the d3-pawn compact. Supports the centre: the pawn on d3 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1.}
 5... Nf6 {Black develops naturally behind the c5-d5-e5 centre. White should not fight it head-on but prepare e4 (after Nbd2) and Re1 to make the centre a target.}
 6. Nbd2 {Supports e4. Develops the knight to d2, where it controls e4.}
 6... Be7 {A modest but safe home for the bishop: it shields the f6-knight from pins and lets Black castle next. Don't worry that it looks passive; it can reroute later.}
 7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] The break that challenges Black's centre. The pawn on e4 controls f5 and eyes the pawn on d5.}
 7... O-O {Black. Castles kingside: the king is safe and the rook on f8 joins the game.}
 8. Re1 {Pressure on e5 and support for e4. The rook goes to e1 and supports the pawn on e4.}
 8... d4 {[%csl Ge4,Rc3] Black closes the centre. White will play a4/Nc4 or prepare f4.})
(2... g6 {A symmetrical setup. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
 3. Bg2 {Fianchetto. The bishop on g2 rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 3... Bg7 {Black contests the long diagonal. Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6, e5 and d4.}
 4. O-O {In this mirror position White is a full move ahead. White castles and will choose between d3/e4 and c4 depending on how Black sets up.}
 4... e5 {Black takes space. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
 5. d3 {Prepares e4. Supports the centre: the pawn on d3 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1.}
 5... Ne7 {Keeps the f-pawn free for ...f5. Develops the knight to e7, where it supports the pawn on d5. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
 6. Nbd2 {Supports e4. Develops the knight to d2, where it controls e4.}
 6... O-O {Black castles behind ...e5 and ...Ne7, a setup that keeps ...f5 possible. White answers with e4 to stop ...f5 grabbing even more space.}
 7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] Challenges the centre. The pawn on e4 controls f5 and eyes the pawn on d5.}
 7... c5 {Black grabs more space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c5 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.})
3. Bg2 {Fianchetto, the backbone of the KIA. The bishop on g2 rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
3... e6 {A French-style setup, the most common way to meet the KIA.}
