4. O-O {King safety first. Castles kingside: the king is safe and the rook on f1 joins the game.}
4... Be7 {A modest but safe home for the bishop: it shields the f6-knight from pins and lets Black castle next. Don't worry that it looks passive; it can reroute later.}
5. d3 {[%csl Ge4] Prepares e4. Supports the centre: the pawn on d3 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1.}
5... O-O {Black castles into a solid Queen's-Gambit-like shell. White's standard plan is Nbd2 and e4, asking whether Black takes, pushes or keeps the tension.}
6. Nbd2 {[%cal Gd2e4] Supports the e4 break. Develops the knight to d2, where it controls e4.}
6... c5 {[%csl Gc5] Black gains queenside space. In the KIA, Black attacks on the queenside and White on the kingside.}
7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] The thematic break. Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls f5 and eyes the pawn on d5.}
7... Nc6 {Black brings the knight to c6 to add weight to d4 and e5. Now e5 by White would gain space and drive the f6-knight away; watch for that.}
8. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5] Backs up e4 and prepares e5. The rook goes to e1 and supports the pawn on e4.}
8... b5 {Black races on the queenside. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on b5 takes a4 and c4 away from the opponent.}
(8... dxe4 {Black releases the central tension. Takes the pawn on e4; White will want to recapture.}
 9. dxe4 {Recaptures. The d-file opens and White's knight can come to c4.}
 9... e5 {Black stakes a claim in the centre. The pawn on e5 controls d4 and f4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
 10. c3 {[%csl Gd4] Takes d4 away from Black's knight. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
 10... Qc7 {The queen goes to c7 and supports the pawn on e5.}
 11. Qe2 {Supports e4 and connects the rooks. The queen goes to e2 and supports the pawn on e4.})
9. e5 {[%csl Gd6,Gf6] The classic KIA wedge: it grabs space and drives the f6-knight away from the kingside.}
9... Nd7 {The knight retreats and eyes e5. The knight goes to d7 and eyes the pawn on e5.}
10. Nf1 {[%cal Gf1h2] The standard regroup: Nf1-h2-g4 or Ne3, joining the kingside attack.}
10... a5 {Black continues the queenside pawn storm. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on a5 takes b4 away from the opponent.}
11. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] White's kingside attack starts. h4 supports Bf4 and N1h2–g4, or Ng5. Black must hurry on the queenside: speed matters more than material here.}
